//? Title: Elias Gamma: Actual Codeword Boundaries and Full-Width Integers
//? Subtitle: Self-delimiting positive integers, exact bit counts and strict native C++ parsing
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Given a sequence of positive integers, we want a binary representation
whose word boundaries can be recovered without separators or a fixed
field width. Small values should use few bits, while occasional large
values must remain representable. Elias gamma writes a zero-prefix
length indicator followed by the value's binary digits. We inspect
actual emitted and parsed spans, retain the original codeword and
mixed-sequence examples, derive its implied probability buckets and
check exact work counts. The core supports every positive uint64 value
and rejects incomplete or nonbinary streams explicitly.
)

\toc

> Begin with thirteen

The binary representation of 13 is 1101, four bits long. Its gamma
word has three zeros followed by those four binary bits: 0001101.
A decoder counts the initial zeros, then consumes the leading one and
three further payload bits. It finishes at the exact codeword boundary.
The following diagram and table read actual spans captured during
encoding and decoding, rather than reconstructing a second explanation.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/elias_gamma_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto e=dm::algorithms::elias_gamma_encode_trace({13});const auto d=dm::algorithms::elias_gamma_decode_trace(e.bits);
  if(d.values!=e.values||e.words.at(0).payload_begin!=d.words.at(0).payload_begin||e.words.at(0).end!=d.words.at(0).end)throw std::runtime_error("span round trip failed");
  std::print("Tiny: value {}; code {:?}; prefix [{},{}); payload [{},{}).\n\n",e.values.at(0),e.bits,e.words.at(0).begin,e.words.at(0).payload_begin,e.words.at(0).payload_begin,e.words.at(0).end);
  std::print("| actual run | values | prefix bits | payload bits | emitted/consumed bits | captured entries |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(const auto& [name,t]:std::vector<std::pair<std::string,dm::algorithms::GammaTrace>>{{"encode",e},{"decode",d}})std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",name,t.work.values,t.work.zero_prefix_bits,t.work.payload_bits,t.work.bit_visits,t.captured_entries);
  dm::graphics::Diagram g(64,1100);dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle box;box.width=1;box.height=1;box.font_size=26;
  const auto& word=e.words.at(0);
  for(std::size_t i=word.begin;i<word.end;++i){box.fill=i<word.payload_begin?dm::graphics::RGB{191,219,254}:dm::graphics::RGB{187,247,208};
    g.node(std::to_string(i),{1.5*i,0},std::string(1,e.bits[i]),box).text({1.5*i,-1},std::to_string(i),17);}
  g.text({1.5,1.5},std::format("prefix: {} zeros",word.payload_begin-word.begin),22)
   .text({6.75,1.5},std::format("payload: {}",e.bits.substr(word.payload_begin,word.end-word.payload_begin)),22)
   .text({4.5,3.1},"Thirteen: length prefix followed by binary payload",26).save_svg("build/elias_gamma_tiny.svg");
}
