\caption(The emitter and strict parser agree on begin zero, payload start three and end seven. The zero prefix encodes a length, while the payload carries the value.)
\alttext(A native row of seven bit cells shows three blue zeros followed by green one-one-zero-one cells, with their actual bit indices and prefix/payload labels.)

> Derive length, boundaries and the positive domain

For n>=1, let k=floor(log2 n), so 2^k<=n<2^(k+1). Write k
zeros and then the k+1 binary digits of n, including its leading one.
The total length is

\[
L(n)=k+(k+1)=2\lfloor\log_2 n\rfloor+1.
\]

The core obtains width from integer bit operations, rather than floating
logarithms or a shift past the word width. For uint64 values, k ranges
from zero to 63 and L from one to 127. Every encoder shift is in
[0,63]. Zero has no gamma codeword in this API; it is rejected rather
than silently disappearing from a sequence.

The first one determines k, and the parser then knows exactly how many
payload bits remain. If two words have different k, the shorter zero
prefix reaches a one while the longer still has a zero. If they have
the same k, their total lengths are equal. Thus no distinct codeword is
a prefix of another, and concatenated complete words have unique
boundaries. Encoding preserves every payload bit, so parsing each such
word recovers its original positive value.

The string API represents each bit by a literal '0' or '1' character.
It uses one char per coded bit, plus string storage overhead. Bit-count
savings below concern a hypothetical binary payload, not packed-byte
compression performed by this API. Message framing and, for a fixed-width
alternative, transmission of its chosen width/count are excluded.

> Preserve the original twelve codewords and mixed sequence

The original report lists integers one through twelve and round-trips
{1,2,5,3,100,7,42,1000,8,9}. Its gamma payload has 74 bits.
The largest value, 1000, needs ten binary digits, so the minimal common
fixed width uses 10·10=100 bits. A declared 16-bit width would use
160 bits; comparing only with that choice overstates the advantage.
The bit-character string itself has 74 characters, not ten packed bytes.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/elias_gamma_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");std::vector<double>x,lengths,fixed;
  std::print("| n | original gamma codeword | exact length |\n| ---: | :--- | ---: |\n");
  for(std::uint64_t n=1;n<=12;++n){const auto code=dm::algorithms::elias_gamma_encode_one(n);std::print("| {} | {} | {} |\n",n,code,dm::algorithms::elias_gamma_code_length(n));x.push_back(n);lengths.push_back(code.size());fixed.push_back(4);}
  const std::vector<std::uint64_t> values{1,2,5,3,100,7,42,1000,8,9};const auto t=dm::algorithms::elias_gamma_encode_trace(values);
  const auto s=dm::algorithms::elias_gamma_size_summary(values);const auto back=dm::algorithms::elias_gamma_decode(t.bits);
  if(back!=values||s.gamma_bits!=74||s.fixed_width!=10||s.fixed_bits!=100)throw std::runtime_error("original sequence identity failed");
  std::print("\nOriginal mixed sequence: {} values; {} gamma bits; minimal fixed width {}; {} fixed bits; declared 16-bit fields {} bits; round trip {}.\n",s.values,s.gamma_bits,s.fixed_width,s.fixed_bits,16*s.values,back==values);
  std::print("Encoded bit characters: {}\n\n",t.bits);
  std::print("| value | prefix start | payload start | word end | actual emitted word |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- |\n");
  for(const auto& w:t.words)std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",w.value,w.begin,w.payload_begin,w.end,t.bits.substr(w.begin,w.end-w.begin));
  auto p=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();p.line(x,lengths,"Gamma",{37,99,235}).points(x,lengths,"",{37,99,235},5).line(x,fixed,"Fixed width 4",{220,38,38})
   .title("Code lengths for the original twelve integers").x_label("Positive integer n").y_label("Payload bits per value").y_limits(0,9).size(950,520).save_svg("build/elias_gamma_original.svg");
}
