For the mixed sequence the payload saving relative to the minimal
fixed width is 100−74=26 bits, or 26%. This example benefits from
many small values and a few large ones. A finite corpus is not evidence
that every integer sequence will obtain the same saving.

> Check the implied probability buckets and Kraft sum

A codeword of length L has implied mass p(n)=2^(-L(n)). In bucket
2^k<=n<2^(k+1), all 2^k values have the same mass

\[
p(n)=2^{-(2k+1)},\qquad
\sum_{n=2^k}^{2^{k+1}-1}p(n)=2^{-(k+1)}.
\]

Summing the geometric bucket masses over unbounded positive integers
gives one, so this infinite prefix code has Kraft sum one. After bucket
K, the partial sum is 1−2^(-(K+1)). The finite uint64 codebook covers
buckets zero through 63, giving mathematical mass 1−2^-64. Binary64
arithmetic cannot resolve that final difference from one; the numerical
checks below stop at K=20, where every displayed dyadic sum is exact.

The mass is a staircase in n, not a smooth inverse square. Specifically,
1/(2n²)<=p(n)<2/n², with equality on the lower bound at powers of two.
Under the constructed unbounded distribution p, mean length is
sum_k (2k+1)2^(-(k+1))=3 bits, equal to its entropy because
-log2 p(n)=L(n). That optimality concerns this particular distribution;
other sources can favour another code.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/elias_gamma_kraft.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");double partial=0;std::vector<double>kvalues,tail,nvalues,log_mass;
  std::print("| bucket k | first n | last n | length | per-value mass | bucket mass | cumulative mass |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(unsigned k=0;k<=20;++k){const auto count=std::uint64_t{1}<<k;const auto length=dm::algorithms::elias_gamma_code_length(count);const double probability=std::ldexp(1.,-int(length)),mass=count*probability;partial+=mass;
    if(length!=2*k+1||mass!=std::ldexp(1.,-int(k)-1)||partial!=1-std::ldexp(1.,-int(k)-1))throw std::runtime_error("Kraft bucket identity failed");
    if(k<=8||k==10||k==15||k==20)std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {:.10g} | {:.10g} | {:.12f} |\n",k,count,2*count-1,length,probability,mass,partial);
    kvalues.push_back(k);tail.push_back(std::log2(1-partial));}
  for(std::uint64_t n=1;n<=64;++n){nvalues.push_back(n);log_mass.push_back(-double(dm::algorithms::elias_gamma_code_length(n)));}
  std::print("\nChecked through bucket 20: cumulative mass {:.12f}; remaining mass {:.12g}.\n",partial,1-partial);
  auto a=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();a.points(nvalues,log_mass,"p(n)",{37,99,235},4).title("Mass is constant within each dyadic bucket").x_label("Positive integer n").y_label("log2(implied mass p(n))").y_limits(-15,1);
  auto b=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();b.line(kvalues,tail,"remaining mass",{124,58,237}).title("Remaining Kraft mass halves each bucket").x_label("Last included bucket K").y_label("log2(remaining Kraft mass)").y_limits(-24,0);
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(a).add(b).size(1100,520).title("Exact dyadic bucket masses and a checked finite Kraft sum").fit_panels().save_svg("build/elias_gamma_kraft.svg");
}
