Maximum spans: prefix [0,63); payload [63,127); actual payload ones 64.
Malformed legacy-stream rejections 8/8; zero-value rejections 2/2; overwide-prefix rejections 1/1.
Exact encoding/decoding entries 9/9; disabled-cap entries 9; base/one-short rejections 3/3.
Empty encoding entries 0; empty decoding values 0.
\image(figures/elias_gamma_boundaries.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The maximum value occupies 127 coded characters and 129 retained trace entries. Strict grammar/domain failures and exact/one-short quotas are exercised above.)
\alttext(A native three-box diagram shows sixty-three zero-prefix bits, sixty-four payload ones and the recovered maximum unsigned sixty-four-bit value.)

> What the examples establish

Actual spans make the self-delimiting boundary visible, and the original
codewords and 74-bit mixed payload remain unchanged. Exact integer width
handles the full positive uint64 domain. Kraft buckets explain the
piecewise probability implied by the code, while controlled fixtures show
that payload savings depend on magnitudes. Strict parsing and explicit
history accounting make incomplete input and resource limits visible;
the character-string representation still needs a separate packing and
framing design for a compact byte-level format.

> Reference

- P. Elias (1975), “Universal codeword sets and representations of the
  integers”, IEEE Transactions on Information Theory 21(2), 194–203,
  [doi:10.1109/TIT.1975.1055349](https://doi.org/10.1109/TIT.1975.1055349).
  [Original paper copy](https://www.u-cursos.cl/ingenieria/2017/2/CC7310/1/material_docente/bajar?bajar=1&id=1879595).
  This implementation uses zero-prefixed gamma words for positive uint64
  values, a strict complete bit-string parser and actual word-span capture.
